\begin_layout Title
Choice Validation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
October 15, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Experimental studies on individual decision making have been primarily concerned
 with how and why complicated models fail.
 These experiments to focus on the different assumptions that individuals
 may violate.
 Fewer experiments are run to discern the way people formulate their preferences
, or how they make decisions.
 With my experiment, I hope to study the phenomenon known as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

choice validation.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 To elaborate, many individuals often find it difficult to make a decision
 when choosing between similar goods, and they may even have indifference
 for the different available options.
 It is not until they receive feedback about the purchasing behavior of
 other agents that they are able to make an actual decision.
 How prevalent is this actual phenomenon, and how much does it effect the
 individual decisions of agents?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In order to analyze the behavior I just described, I will recruit subjects
 to choose between two similar items obtained from the Texas A&M school
 store, such as coffee mugs, pens, etc.
 Each subject will meet with the experimenter individually, a set of 10
 pairs of similar items will be presented, and the subject will be required
 to make a decision on which could he would like to keep.
 The controlled experiment will require one group of subjects to choose
 an item without any outside assistance.
 Also, before a decision is made, the experimenter will inquire whether
 the subject is indifferent between the two goods, and record the response.
 Then, another group of cohorts will be recruited to participate in the
 treatment experiment.
 Now, after a pair of items is presented to a subject, the experimenter
 will inform the subject which item was more popular, i.e., which item had
 higher sales and/or was chosen more in the previous experiment, instead
 of asking if the subject is indifferent between the two.
\end_layout

